\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Which exponent was obtained}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{The limit in \ref{fdip-00F5} is the eigenvalue-counting
exponent of the cited Laplacian. \ref{fdip-00F6} identifies
that Dirichlet spectrum with the generator on #{F_0}. For
the Neumann condition, \citet{Theorem 3.11}{kigami1996kokyuroku}
puts each cited eigenfunction into the domain of #{H_\mu}.
The converse inclusion is not proved. The lattice clause
leaves a bounded periodic factor in
#{\rho_*(\Lambda)/\Lambda^{d_s/2}}. The 1996 statement does not
evaluate that factor as a constant. The exponent is distinct
from a logarithmic derivative of a heat trace, from the
Euclidean Hausdorff dimension #{\log 3/\log 2} recorded in
\citet{Theorem 4.4}{kigami1996kokyuroku}, and from the
resistance Hausdorff dimension #{\log 3/(\log 5-\log 3)} in
\citet{Theorem 4.12}{kigami1996kokyuroku}, which uses another
measure. The algebraic
equation with #{r_i=\mu_i=1/q} yields #{d_s=1}. The
homeomorphism of \ref{fdip-00EU} is the identification of
the underlying space. An exponent common to a class of
conductances is not proved.}
